---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="The line"
      eyebrow="The spine, generation by generation"
      dek="The descent from Pablo Armando is closed and documented, with no inferred link left in it. And as of 10 September 2026 the line runs one generation higher: an Argentine army card names his father and mother.">
<section class="blk">

<div class="panel" style="margin:1.4rem 0">
    <p style="margin:0">
      Two things shape this page. <strong>Living people are omitted from the build</strong>, which removes
      the most recent generations. And the top of the line changed on <strong>10 September 2026</strong>:
      an Argentine army enrolment card, filled in at the consulate in <strong>Cape Town</strong>, names
      Pablo Armando&rsquo;s father and mother. For months there was nothing above him. There is now one
      generation, and it is documented rather than inferred.
      <a href={u("/enrolment-card/")}>The card &rarr;</a> &middot;
      <a href={u("/method")}>Method &rarr;</a> &middot; <a href={u("/searched")}>What has been searched &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>The line</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Gen</th><th>Person</th><th>Dates</th><th>Where</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">0</td>
          <td><strong>Juan Carlos Lerena</strong><br />
            <span class="cap">m. <strong>María Lerena</strong> &mdash; so written on the card;
            her maiden name is not yet known</span></td>
          <td class="mono">&mdash;</td>
          <td>Presumed <strong>Rosario de Santa Fe</strong>; nothing else yet known of him</td>
          <td><span class="chip doc">Documented</span><br /><span class="cap">Named as the father on his
            son&rsquo;s <a href={u("/enrolment-card/")}>1908 <em>ficha de enrolamiento</em></a>. Corroborated
            from outside Argentina: Pablo Armando&rsquo;s eldest son was
            <strong>Ricardo <em>Juan Carlos</em> Lerena</strong>, b. 6 Jan 1920, Cape Town.</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">1</td>
          <td><strong>Pablo Armando Lerena</strong><br />
            <span class="cap">in South Africa <strong>Robert Paul Lerena</strong><br />
            m. <strong>Mary Septima Taylor</strong>, d. Nov 1935 aged 51</span></td>
          <td class="mono">22 Feb 1882 &ndash; 19 Mar 1950</td>
          <td>Born <strong>Rosario de Santa Fe</strong>, Argentina; died at Newlands, Cape Town</td>
          <td><span class="chip doc">Documented</span><br /><span class="cap">Argentine passport, 1940:
            born Rosario, <strong>Trainer</strong>. Death certificate A 214036:
            <strong>Race Horse Owner</strong>. Named as father in the 1911 Corpus Christi baptism of his
            son Roque.</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">2</td>
          <td><strong>Nuno Fernando Lerena</strong><br />
            <span class="cap">m. Catherine Mary Sophia <strong>Booyzen</strong>, 1929&ndash;2013,
            at Johannesburg</span></td>
          <td class="mono">6 Oct 1924 &ndash; 1 Jul 1971</td>
          <td>Born <strong>Cape Town</strong>; died at Germiston, Transvaal</td>
          <td><span class="chip doc">Documented</span><br /><span class="cap">Named with this exact birth
            date as a residuary heir in his father's 1950 estate; own death notice 6746/71. A
            salesman.</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono">3</td>
          <td><strong>Cheryl Anne Lerena</strong></td>
          <td class="mono">b. 14 Oct 1955</td>
          <td>Born Johannesburg; later Brisbane</td>
          <td><span class="chip">Omitted</span><br /><span class="cap">Named among her father's children
            in his 1971 death notice. Details withheld under the rule on living people.</span></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>Every step of this line now rests on a record.</strong>
    Nuno Fernando's birth date appears in his father's estate papers and again on his own death notice;
    Cheryl is named in that death notice as his daughter. There is no inferred link left in the descent.
    <a href={u("/estate")}>The estate &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>Her line goes back further than his</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> Pablo Armando's wife was <strong>Mary Septima Taylor</strong>
    (17 September 1884 &ndash; 15 November 1935), and her family can be traced <strong>three generations
    above her</strong>, to a <strong>James Taylor born about 1796</strong> — further back than the Lerena
    side, where Pablo Armando's own parents remain unknown.
  </p>
  <p>
    Her name is a count: she was the <strong>seventh daughter</strong> of ten children, and
    <em>Septima</em> means seventh. <a href={u("/taylor")}>The Taylors &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>Generation 1's other children</h2>
  <p>Pablo Armando Lerena and Mary Septima Taylor had at least three sons, all born at the Cape.</p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Son</th><th>Born</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Roque Luis Armando Lerena</strong></td><td class="mono">25 Nov 1905, Cape Town</td>
          <td>Baptised at Corpus Christi, Wynberg, 12 June 1911. Retired railwayman; d. Johannesburg
            29 Jan 1973. The "Louis" of the 1950 will. <strong>His baptism and his death notice between
            them identify generation 1</strong> — and prove that Pablo Armando and Robert Paul are one man.
            <span class="cap">His death notice gives 6 Dec 1905; the register, 25 Nov.</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Ricardo Juan Carlos Lerena</strong></td><td class="mono">6 Jan 1920</td>
          <td>Residuary heir with Nuno. His descendants are the largest branch on the family chart.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Nuno Fernando Lerena</strong></td><td class="mono">6 Oct 1924, Cape Town</td>
          <td>David Defranceski's grandfather.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="cap">
    Cheryl's own brothers and sisters — Jeannette Marie, Paul Raymond, Catherine Tertia and Patricia
    Carmen — are named in their father's 1971 death notice and are all now deceased. Note
    <strong>Paul</strong> descending from generation 1, and <strong>Carmen</strong> surviving as a given
    name into 1958.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>Gavin Lerena</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip">Family chart</span> This archive earlier recorded the South African jockey
    <strong>Gavin Lerena</strong> as a public figure and expressly claimed no relationship. The family's own
    descendant chart now places him: son of <strong>Carlos "Tex" Lerena</strong>, son of
    <strong>Ricardo</strong>, son of <strong>Pablo Armando</strong> — which would make him Cheryl's first
    cousin once removed, and David's second cousin.
  </p>
  <p class="cap">
    Recorded as the family holds it, and not yet verified against a public record. It is, however, exactly
    what this archive predicted before any of it was known: that the
    <a href={u("/south-africa")}>sixty-one South African Lerenas are one kindred</a>, and that a rare
    surname plus a shared trade is a strong signal.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>Where it joins the other archives</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cheryl Anne Lerena married Ivan Defranceski.</strong> That marriage is the hinge between this
    archive and the <em>Defranceski</em> archive, whose line runs back through Johannesburg to Gračišće and
    the Istrian peninsula. Their son married into the <em>Falco</em> line of Arienzo in Campania.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three archives, three continents of origin, meeting in Brisbane: <strong>Lerena</strong> from the River
    Plate by way of South Africa, <strong>Defranceski</strong> from Istria, <strong>Falco</strong> from
    Campania.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>What is still missing</h2>
  <p>
    The line is solid downward from Pablo Armando Lerena, and his own birth is fixed to a city and a day by
    his own passport — <strong>Rosario de Santa Fe, 22 February 1882</strong>. <strong>Above him there is
    nothing.</strong> His parents are unnamed in every document held here.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>And the obvious way to supply them has now been closed.</strong> The Rosario cathedral baptism
    index has been read letter by letter, under <em>any</em> surname, for <strong>1881, 1882 and
    1883</strong> — twenty-six sections in each year, the cathedral being the city&rsquo;s only parish until
    1888 — and he is in none of it. The marriage registers are clear from <strong>1868 to 1891</strong>.
    Uruguayan civil registration, which began in 1879, does not hold him either; nor do the indexed Spanish
    parish records.
    <a href={u("/searched")}>The full record of what has been searched &rarr;</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    So the hope has moved off birth records altogether, onto <strong>three letters now with archives</strong>:
    his <strong>military enrolment</strong> in Buenos Aires, which would name his parents outright; the
    <strong>consular file</strong> behind his 1940 passport, which would show what he produced to prove he
    was Argentine; and the <strong>anarchist press in Amsterdam</strong>, which named the men deported from
    Argentina in the years he left.
  </p>
  <p><a href={u("/open-questions")}>Open questions &rarr;</a></p>
</section>
</Base>
